From 383f80f21f16c3c94e7d7a57b0a5af12cdac9d75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rainer Gerhards <rgerhards@adiscon.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 16:23:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] imjournal: stop invalidation reopen busy-loop

Why:
Journal invalidation recovery introduced in 8.2606.0 can consume a
full CPU core while repeatedly reopening an otherwise idle journal.

Impact:
imjournal blocks normally after journal rotation or invalidation instead
of spinning and flooding the internal log.

Before/After:
Before, each reopened handle immediately triggered another invalidation;
after, the handle initializes its notification state before resuming.

Technical Overview:
Prime each reopened sd-journal handle with a zero-timeout wait.
This installs and consumes the fresh handle notification state.
Treat negative wait results as recovery errors.
Restore the saved cursor only after notification initialization.
Preserve the reopen that protects invalidated mappings from reuse.
Document the operator-visible fix in the 8.2608 ChangeLog.

Closes https://github.com/rsyslog/rsyslog/issues/7342

With the help of AI-Agents: Codex

Upstream-Status: Backport [https://github.com/rsyslog/rsyslog/commit/383f80f21f16c3c94e7d7a57b0a5af12cdac9d75]
---
--- a/plugins/imjournal/imjournal.c
+++ b/plugins/imjournal/imjournal.c
@@ -853,10 +853,24 @@
 
 static rsRetVal reopenJournal(struct journalContext_s *journalContext) {
     DEFiRet;
+    int waitRet;
 
     closeJournal(journalContext);
     CHKiRet(openJournal(journalContext));
 
+    /* The first sd_journal_wait() on a newly opened handle intentionally
+     * returns immediately after installing its watches. Consume that initial
+     * state here; otherwise an INVALIDATE-triggered reopen causes every fresh
+     * handle to report another INVALIDATE and imjournal busy-loops forever.
+     * Cursor restoration follows this call, so sequential delivery still
+     * resumes from the last processed entry. */
+    waitRet = sd_journal_wait(journalContext->j, 0);
+    if (waitRet < 0) {
+        LogError(-waitRet, RS_RET_ERR, "imjournal: sd_journal_wait() failed while initializing reopened journal");
+        closeJournal(journalContext);
+        ABORT_FINALIZE(RS_RET_ERR);
+    }
+
 finalize_it:
     RETiRet;
 }
